Changelog: 22-10-2026 19:31:16

Release 0.2.0.

Changelog: 22-10-2026 18:09:37

Added. Pictures from SteamGridDB for the games in Steam (owner, 22-10-2026), with core's covers. A cartridge dump
brings no pictures, so all four were always drawn from the app's icon and the game's title. With the suite's
SteamGridDB API key, entered under the new Covers… button (one key for every app of the suite, kept in
~/.config/bc250-bazzite-suite/steamgriddb-key), Update Game Mode first looks each ticked game up by its title, without
the tags of its file name ("Tetris (World) (Rev 1)" as "Tetris"), and puts the cover, the wide picture, the banner and
the logo SteamGridDB has in place of the drawn ones; one it lacks stays drawn. The pictures are kept in
~/.local/share/bc250-sameboy-manager/covers under a name from the game's title, so a game is asked for once; a game in
Steam not yet looked up turns Update Game Mode on. A refused key or no network stops the lookup with a message and the
shortcuts are written all the same. Without a key nothing changes. The artwork is now drawn in the background job,
after the lookup. install.sh --uninstall --purge removes the covers folder too; the installer test checks it. Test: the pictures in place of the drawn ones, a file name's tags left out of the lookup, and
Covers… with Update Game Mode in the window.

Changelog: 21-10-2026 14:25:29

Release 0.1.0.

Changelog: 21-10-2026 13:23:31

Added. SameBoy Manager: Game Boy and Game Boy Color games on the BC-250 with the SameBoy core of RetroArch (SameBoy
releases no Linux build). As SwanStation Manager does, the app installs RetroArch from Flathub (a Flatpak, for the user,
or uses the one that is installed) and the core from the libretro build server (checked as a zip with one x86-64 Linux
library; the build server keeps only the newest build, so the core is not pinned: the app records its build date and
SHA-256, says when there is a newer build, also in the portal's notice of updates outside the suite, and installs it on
Update). No BIOS is needed. It finds the games in the chosen folders (.gb and .gbc files, and .zip files with one in
them, told by the cartridge header's checksum and ROM size, the system from its colour flag) and puts the ticked ones in
Steam for Game Mode, "[GB] Title" or "[GBC] Title", the artwork drawn from the app's icon. The Game Mode shortcuts run
~/.local/bin/bc250-sameboy-run, which starts the RetroArch Flatpak with the core (--fullscreen) and, when Steam starts a
game on a Wayland desktop, without RetroArch's Wayland socket so Steam's overlay works. Not tried on the board yet: a
game from Game Mode, a zipped game, the controllers under Steam Input.
